Here's a function in Python to find the longest consecutive sequence of 1s in the binary representation of a number:

```python
def longest_binary_ones(n):
    max_ones = 0
    current_ones = 0
    while n:
        if n & 1:
            current_ones += 1
            if current_ones > max_ones:
                max_ones = current_ones
        else:
            current_ones = 0
        n >>= 1
    return max_ones
```

This function uses bitwise operations to check each bit of the number, incrementing counters when a 1 is found, and resetting them when a 0 is encountered, keeping track of the maximum number of consecutive 1s.

Here's a C++ function that merges two sorted lists into a single sorted list:

```cpp
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> merge(const std::vector<int>& list1, const std::vector<int>& list2) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    size_t i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < list1.size() && j < list2.size()) {
        if (list1[i] <= list2[j]) {
            result.push_back(list1[i]);
            ++i;
        } else {
            result.push_back(list2[j]);
            ++j;
        }
    }
    // Append remaining elements
    while (i < list1.size()) {
        result.push_back(list1[i]);
        ++i;
    }
    while (j < list2.size()) {
        result.push_back(list2[j]);
        ++j;
    }
    return result;
}
```

This function uses a two-pointer technique to traverse both input lists and pushes the smaller current element to the result list. After exhausting one of the lists, it appends the remaining elements from the other list.

